
        
        <p>This week, it's been exciting to introduce two health-focused iOS apps: <a href="https://one37.net/2026/11/introducing-breakfast.html">Breakfast</a> and <a href="https://one37.net/daybreak/">Daybreak</a>.</p>
<p>As a quick recap: Breakfast is a running analysis app for iPhone and iPad. Daybreak exports a subset of your Apple Health data to JSON and CSV files, allowing you to analyse your trends using tools of your choosing.</p>
<p>Now, for some added context on it all.</p>
<p>I started this blog 15 years ago. (!) I was a year out of university and, while I was fortunate to have landed a good job, I felt stifled by corporate American life.</p>
<p>I had always cared deeply about technology and design. I was largely self-taught across both (plus some Computer Science at university), but had ambitions to do more in that world. So, I started writing about the space and following developers, designers, and entrepreneurs I found inspiring.</p>
<p>In the time since, I've continued to closely follow the indie community around the Apple ecosystem. That was the core basis for this blog in its most active years, as well as Bionic. (Less so Bonanza!)</p>
<p>I've bought a handful of books and embarked on a variety of courses to learn to develop iOS apps. But, between building my own companies, starting a family, and everything else, I never got anything over the line. (My primary ambition was always to develop an email app, which, in retrospect, is probably why I was never able to make any progress!)</p>
<p>I became a father in 2021. In parallel, I was deep in the throes of building my last company and, coming off the back of the pandemic, was in the worst health of my life.</p>
<p>At the age of 34, I weighed in at 215 lbs (97.5 kg) in January 2022. For a 5'8&quot; (172 cm) man, that put me firmly in the obese category.</p>
<p>I had tried and failed to establish an exercise habit in the preceding years. I'd sporadically found some form of rhythm, but it'd be easily derailed by my work obligations.</p>
<p>I'd walked frequently during the earliest days of the pandemic, which I loved. But it was time-consuming and, living in Texas, often unsustainably hot. (Or cold.) So running felt like an intuitive option. (Walking, but faster!)</p>
<p>I started trying to run in 2021, but ended up with shin splints. I'd struggled with a familiar problem: when running, I felt that I should be going quickly. It was more exciting that way. And it felt like I was working harder, which seemed like it'd help with weight loss.</p>
<p>Generally, this would result in injury or burning out before I really moved the fitness needle. The habit would fall away.</p>
<p>But, in 2022, I picked it back up with Nike Run Club's guided audio runs. I followed its 5K program and Coach Bennett calmly and repeatedly reminded me to do one thing: slow down. (I haven't used NRC in years, but I still have such appreciation for Coach Bennett.)</p>
<p>I recognise this sounds relatively obvious, but it blew open running for me. I gave myself permission to run very slowly and gradually build distance.</p>
<p>I recall running my first uninterrupted 5K in May 2022, which was an enormously empowering and exciting moment. By November, I was running my first race, a local 10K. I remember weighing in at 184 lbs that day. 31 lbs down from my peak.</p>
<p>Over the ensuing years, the habit continued to compound. I signed up for local races to have regular targets. And, as I sought to improve my performance, I started to improve my broader lifestyle.</p>
<p>After struggling during my first marathon, I significantly cut back on alcohol. Which led to improving my diet. Which led to building a meditation habit. Which led to building a strength training habit too.</p>
<p>Today, I weigh around 140 lbs (63.5 kg). I've lost about 75 lbs (34 kg) since 2022. Body fat at the time of writing is sitting somewhere around 12.5%, down from around 42% in 2022. (!) I enjoy alcohol sporadically. I have a much improved diet. And, thanks to the gym work, I'm becoming a faster and faster runner.</p>
<p>In the first two weeks of November 2026, I've set 11 new personal bests with my running. This includes a 39:17 10K and a 1:28 half marathon. According to Breakfast, I've set 41 personal bests in 2026 so far.</p>
<p>Along this journey, I've found that keeping close track of these milestones, along with a lot of granular metrics, has been critical to maintaining momentum.</p>
<p>I've been wearing an Apple Watch consistently since 2015 and using a Withings scale since 2016. So I've never lacked for data. But building a habit of manually logging key metrics in spreadsheets, journal entries, and the like has been crucial.</p>
<p>Today, I write down my weight, muscle mass, and fat, along with sleep, alcohol intake, gym volume, and running distance in a Numbers spreadsheet. I do this every day, manually. In parallel, I use a (&quot;smart&quot;) tape measure to take body measurements every 2-4 weeks.</p>
<p>Regardless of the apps and tools I'm using, this has kept me honest. It's forced me to be disciplined about regular weigh-ins and, equally, become more flexible about noisy data.</p>
<p>A 'bad' weigh-in used to totally derail me. Now, I understand the patterns. And the averages become much more helpful than those daily readings.</p>
<p>As a student of the indie Apple community, I've always sought to use the best, most thoughtful tools available. I've used Foodnoms throughout this journey to track my diet, for example. Between that, my Apple Watch, a Withings scale, and a Renpho smart tape measure, I've built up a wealth of historical data about my sleep, running, weight, diet, and beyond.</p>
<p>With the rise of tools like ChatGPT and Claude, I'd wanted a way to better analyse patterns in this information. But I'd been wary of doing so without privacy guardrails. Giving an AI service direct, ongoing access to Apple Health was something I was reluctant to do.</p>
<p>So, in late 2025, I decided to see if this was something I could address. Working with ChatGPT and Claude, I built a small tool called LifeiOS, which synced a narrow subset of Apple Health data into daily JSON and CSV exports in iCloud Drive.</p>
<p>Alongside my daily Numbers entries, I'd confirm I was current with Foodnoms, then run a retroactive sync each morning. I could then have a recurring discussion with ChatGPT or a local LLM to identify trends that might not otherwise be obvious.</p>
<p>I was able to quickly see areas of opportunity with my diet, potential correlations between running habits and injuries, and so on.</p>
<p>Feeling empowered by this, I decided to explore something a bit more sophisticated: a great running analysis app for iOS. Something private and restrained, providing an analysis framework for my current and historical exercise data in Apple Health.</p>
<p>I'd previously been flirting with the concept of developing my own running apparel/headwear brand, for which I'd hatched the name Breakfast Running Club. This had been sitting dormant for over a year, so I decided to give it some purpose across both apps.</p>
<p>By mid-2026, I was using Breakfast for all of my running analysis. And I renamed LifeiOS to Daybreak, following the breakfast and morning-time reflection theme. (Break your fast. Get it!? But also exercising in the morning. Get it?!)</p>
<p>I hadn't planned to publish either app. But, when talking to friends about my running, I'd pull up Breakfast to show my latest personal bests. As I continued to chip away at the experience, more people would ask about using it. Turns out: a lot of people dislike Strava.</p>
<p>Like me, they were tired of bloated social experiences and expensive subscriptions. Most were looking for something simpler to better understand their performance. And many were interested in a more controlled approach to AI analysis too. But I remained undecided about publishing.</p>
<p>Over the summer, I was sketching out potential UI elements in my spare time, thinking about how an iPadOS app might look. (I assumed that's how the Duo would operate!)</p>
<p>It was the arrival of the iPhone Duo that put me over the edge. The new form factor opened up some interesting possibilities for visualising running performance. It really captured my imagination.</p>
<p>With that, I launched Breakfast on the App Store on November 7. And, energised by that, I opted to launch Daybreak a week later.</p>
<p>I've been thrilled by the process. After following along with the indie world for years, it's been so exciting to play in the sandbox.</p>
<p>The process has been AI-assisted on the code front, which conjures a lot of feelings. But, for me, it's allowed me to build something that looks and feels exactly how I'd want. I'm proud of the result, which has required a lot of effort and iteration over the course of the year.</p>
<p>Looking ahead, I have a lot of ambition on this front, particularly for Breakfast. I'm aiming to develop responsive training programs. Something modern and thoughtful to help you start out running, train for your next race, build your pace, or otherwise. And to do so with a more thoughtful UI/UX and accessible price-point than what's available in the marketplace.</p>
<p>I'm running two marathons (maybe more!) in 2027 before I turn 40 and intend to rely heavily on my own tools. My last marathon went poorly in 2025, so I'm excited to head into my London 2027 training block with these tools and see where it leads from there.</p>
<p>Please check out both apps if they're of interest to you. And feel free to reach out if you have any thoughts or feedback.</p>

      